    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
      <!-- Card 1: Combustion Kinetics & CO -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">1. Combustion Kinetics & CO Generation</h4>
        <p class="mb-2">
          Natural gas and propane burners require stoichiometric oxygen to oxidize hydrocarbons to carbon dioxide:
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono bg-canvas p-2 rounded-md border border-hairline text-ink text-[11px] mb-2">
          CH₄ + 2(1 + e)O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O + eO₂ + [CO]
        </div>
        <p>
          When the excess air ratio λ drops below 1.0 (sub-stoichiometric), thermal dissociation and oxygen starvation halt the second oxidation stage (CO + OH* → CO2 + H*). Flue gas carbon monoxide escalates exponentially according to power-law kinetics, exceeding 800+ ppm in choked stacks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 2: Chaff Dust Explosibility (Kst) -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">2. Chaff Dust Explosibility & Kst Metric</h4>
        <p class="mb-2">
          Organic chaff silver skin fragments exhibit high specific surface area. Suspended dust clouds burn at deflagration velocities governed by the cubic law:
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono bg-canvas p-2 rounded-md border border-hairline text-ink text-[11px] mb-2">
          K_st = (dP/dt)_max · V^(1/3) [bar·m/s]
        </div>
        <p>
          Grain and coffee chaff exhibit Kst values between 150 and 220 bar·m/s (St 1 to St 2). When concentration in cyclone ducting exceeds the Minimum Explosible Concentration (MEC ≈ 40-50 g/m³), an in-flight burner spark triggers instantaneous pressure rise exceeding vessel yield limits.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 3: Duct Transport & Saltation Limits -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">3. Pneumatic Saltation & Settling Limits</h4>
        <p class="mb-2">
          Lightweight chaff flakes must remain aerodynamically suspended in pneumatic exhaust ductwork. The minimum saltation velocity is modeled by:
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono bg-canvas p-2 rounded-md border border-hairline text-ink text-[11px] mb-2">
          V_salt = 1.25 · √(g · D_duct · (ρ_chaff / ρ_air))
        </div>
        <p>
          If transport velocity falls below 14.0–16.0 m/s due to blower wear or damper restriction, chaff settles out onto horizontal duct inverts. Settled dust beds insulate duct steel, absorb pyrolysis oil tar, and undergo exothermic smoldering autoignition.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 4: NFPA 68 Relief Vent Sizing -->
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">4. NFPA 68 Deflagration Vent Area Sizing</h4>
        <p class="mb-2">
          To prevent catastrophic cyclone shell rupture, NFPA 68 defines the required vent relief panel area (Av):
        </p>
        <div class="font-mono bg-canvas p-2 rounded-md border border-hairline text-ink text-[11px] mb-2">
          A_v = 1.0·10⁻⁴ · (1 + 1.54·P_stat^0.5) · K_st · V^0.75 / √P_red
        </div>
        <p>
          For a cyclone volume of 1.8 m³ handling St 1 chaff (Kst = 165) with a design pressure Pred = 0.5 bar, an explosion relief vent of at least 0.32 m² must discharge unhindered to an exterior safe zone with zero occupant exposure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

